  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 132 PM CDT Sat Aug 8 2026\n\nHot Sunday through Wednesday:\n\nTemperatures will increase further on Sunday and into the work \nweek as the aforementioned ridge expands east. In fact,\ntemperatures may approach the 100 degree mark across extreme\nsouthwest Missouri and southeast Kansas each day. There is\ngreater uncertainty in the dew point forecast, however. Stronger\nwinds will support better mixing next week compared to today,\nwhich may act to lower dew points and, ultimately, maximum\nafternoon heat indices by a few degrees, particularly atop the\nplateau. Therefore, the hottest conditions will occur in lower\nelevations where mixing is less of a factor. Confidence is \nmedium to high (40-80% chance) that locations in southwest \nMissouri and southeast Kansas will reach at least a 100 deg heat\nindex tomorrow through Wednesday, resulting in a prolonged heat\nrisk. Confidence in reaching 105 degrees each day is lower, \nhowever, with the greatest chance north of the plateau where an \nExtreme Heat Watch is currently in effect. All this to say that \nwe have held off on any additional headline issuance with this \npackage until forecast confidence increases. \n\nRegardless of exact headlines, it will be hot Sunday\nthrough Wednesday, and those with outdoor plans should make\nefforts to have cooling and hydration options readily available.\nFurthermore, the NWS HeatRisk product depicts increasingly\nwidespread values in the Major category throughout the week.\nThis category means that heat affects anyone without effective\ncooling and/or adequate hydration, and impacts are likely in\nsome health systems, heat-sensitive industries, and\ninfrastructure. \n\nRain Chances Mid to Late Week:\n\nGlobal ensembles depict the upper-level height pattern \nflattening into more of a zonal flow by Wednesday or even \ndigging into a trough across the Midwest and eastern CONUS. The\ndifferences and details will in fact matter when it comes to \nthe sensible weather, but at the very least, this shift will \nopen the door to rain chances for the latter half of the week. \nIndeed, precipitation chances increase to 30-50% each day \nbeginning late Wednesday. The highest chances are across central\nMissouri where synoptic-scale ascent will be the greatest. At \nthe same time, confidence in the temperature forecast decreases.\nNBM interquartile temperature spreads increase from 3 degrees on\nWednesday to 10 degrees on Friday, which exemplifies this \nincreasing uncertainty nicely.",
